<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ludovico Ariosto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1474">1474</a>–<a href="/years/1533">1533</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ludovico Ariosto was an Italian poet at the court of the Este in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>. His romantic epic Orlando Furioso, first published in 1516 and expanded until 1532, continued Boiardo's tale of Orlando and became one of the most influential poems of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. He also wrote comedies and satires and served the Este as governor of the Garfagnana.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ludovico-ariosto.jpg" alt="Ludovico Ariosto" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ludovico_Ariosto_1532.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ludovico Ariosto was born on 8 September 1474 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reggio-emilia">Reggio nell'Emilia</a>, where his father Niccolò Ariosto commanded the citadel. He was the eldest of ten children. He was interested in poetry from his earliest years, but his father obliged him to study law. After five years he was allowed to read the classics under Gregorio da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a>, until his teacher left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and, soon after, his father died. Ariosto then had to give up his literary pursuits and care for a family whose affairs were in disarray, though he still wrote comedies in prose and some lyrical pieces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His writings brought him to the notice of Cardinal Ippolito d'Este, who took him into his household. Ariosto later said the cardinal paid him poorly: the only reward for Orlando Furioso, which was dedicated to him, was the question where he had found so many stories. When the cardinal went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in 1518 and wished Ariosto to go with him, the poet pleaded ill health and the care of his elderly mother, and after a heated argument he was dismissed.</p><p>The cardinal's brother Alfonso, duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, then took him on. Ariosto had already made his name as a diplomat on two embassies to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, on one of which he was nearly killed on the pope's orders, because the pope was then in conflict with Alfonso. His salary of 84 crowns a year was suspended during the war and withdrawn after the peace. He was appointed governor of the Garfagnana, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a>, for three years. The province was troubled by factions and bandits and the duke gave him little support, but both ruler and people were satisfied with his government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His comedy Cassaria appeared in 1508 and I suppositi was first acted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>. Shakespeare later used it as a source for The Taming of the Shrew. The first version of Orlando Furioso, in 40 cantos, was published at Ferrara in 1516, and the third and final version, in 46 cantos, appeared on 8 September 1532. The poem describes the adventures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, Orlando and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> against the Saracens, with many side plots, and turns the chivalric tradition into satire. It is in ottava rima, and the narrator often breaks off a plot in mid-canto and takes it up much later. Goethe called it a graceful, smiling, fantastic creation. Ariosto died on 6 July 1533.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ludovico Ariosto” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ferrara supported the poets Boiardo, <b>Ariosto</b> and Tasso and the painters of the Schifanoia frescoes.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-ferrara">Duchy of Ferrara</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reggio-emilia">Reggio Emilia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.7,10.63&amp;year=1512" aria-label="Reggio Emilia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ludovico Ariosto"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5643 3727h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5643 3727h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.7,10.63&amp;year=1512">What was here in 1512? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;OF LOVES and LADIES, KNIGHTS and ARMS, I sing, Of COURTESIES, and many a DARING FEAT; And from those ancient days my story bring, When Moors from Afric passed in hostile fleet…&#8221;</p><footer>Orlando Furioso, canto 1 (the opening) &#183; tr. William Stewart Rose, 1823 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/615/pg615-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;In the same strain of Roland will I tell Things unattempted yet in prose or rhyme, On whom strange madness and rank fury fell, A man esteemed so wise in former time…&#8221;</p><footer>Orlando Furioso, canto 1 &#183; tr. William Stewart Rose, 1823 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/615/pg615-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
